WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:05.520
Here's a finished placket opening
that could be used for a Henley style

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:10.520
sweater or for the back of a child's
sweater for more head room.

3
00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:15.840
The edges of the opening are folded
back for a facing on each side.

4
00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:20.720
It overlaps with four layers of knitting.
With careful finishing,

5
00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:24.120
it looks great.
Even when the placket flips open.

6
00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.480
You could work buttonholes or you might

7
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:31.640
consider snaps or poppers,
if that's what you call them.

8
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.000
The knitting is fairly straightforward,
but you have to take it step-by-step.

9
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:43.800
I highly suggest that you knit a sample (or
two) before adding a placket to a sweater.

10
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.960
And when you're ready to knit your

11
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:52.840
sweater, add a lifeline before starting
the placket, opening just in case.

12
00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.480
For our sample, on the standard gauge

13
00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.800
machine, we cast-on 50 stitches
and we knit for about 30 rows.

14
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.520
This represents the body of the sweater.

15
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.480
I'm creating a 6 stitch placket.

16
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:12.080
When it's time to split the opening,
I end with the carriage

17
00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.320
on the left.
We'll remove the yarn from the yarn feeder

18
00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:20.080
and secure it out of the way so it
doesn't get caught up in the carriage.

19
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.760
Just leave it there for now

20
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:29.760
with no yarn in the carriage free pass
across to the right. Use your slip setting.

21
00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:34.600
Put the left side,

22
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:40.120
that's all of the needles, all 25  needles into hold position.

23
00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:49.320
Scrap off the right side of the knitting.

24
00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.560
Just drop that side off the machine.

25
00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.760
Free pass to the left.

26
00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.600
This time the needles are in hold so you

27
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.960
can use the hold setting
of your carriage to free pass.

28
00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:09.280
We want to get that carriage back
to where the working yarn is waiting.

29
00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:14.560
Put the empty needles in the right out

30
00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:17.240
of work, we're going to just
be working on the left

31
00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.080
25 needles.

32
00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:24.480
Thread up the main yarn
and knit to the right.

33
00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:30.080
Now, here comes a bit of a tricky part.
We're going to cast-on for our placket

34
00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:36.320
extension, but first we're going
to take an extra step to prevent a hole.

35
00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:41.520
Before doing anything else,
pick up the last stitch of the scraped off

36
00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:47.400
section and place it on the first
needle of the section in work.

37
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.600
Notice that I placed that stitch
under the working yarn.

38
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:59.440
Now pull out 12 needles and e-wrap
cast-on over those needles.

39
00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:11.080
You may want to use a Cast-on rag
and a row of Ravel cord to hang a weight.

40
00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.760
Sometimes Cast-on with just
an e-wrap can be difficult.

41
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:22.840
Knit across, making sure that those
cast-on stitches knit properly.

42
00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:39.120
Knit for about 30 rows.

43
00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:47.280
The first side is complete.

44
00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:55.640
Notice the Cast-on stitches and the
scraped off edge. Looking at our drawing,

45
00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.760
this fold will create the facing

46
00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:04.200
and this is going to be
the center front of the sweater.

47
00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.520
Next, we'll knit the other
side of the placket.

48
00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.600
Be sure the carriage is on the right.

49
00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.880
Just to keep myself oriented,

50
00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.240
I pinned the first facing in place.

51
00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:26.040
Pick up the twenty five 25  the waste yarn.

52
00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.360
And here's the tricky part again.

53
00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:36.040
Remember when we picked up the first
stitch to prevent that hole?

54
00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:40.760
Be sure to pick up that stitch again,
it may be a little difficult to see,

55
00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:47.920
but be sure you get the entire stitch, the
entire strand and hang it on the needle.

56
00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.880
Next, will join the facing and the opening

57
00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:54.800
of that first side to this
side and complete the placket.

58
00:04:56.120 --> 00:05:02.400
Fold the Erap edge of the facing
and line up the first six stitches.

59
00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.200
Hang the  e-wraps on the needles.

60
00:05:54.640 --> 00:06:00.280
Next we will pick up the remaining e-wraps
and hang them on the same needles.

61
00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.080
There are three stitches on each

62
00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:26.360
of the six needles,
two strands from the first folded placket

63
00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:31.400
and a strand or a stitch
from the waste yarn.

64
00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:35.080
Folded over, notice that the knit side

65
00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:39.920
of that first section is
facing me and the fold,

66
00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.160
it's on the right.

67
00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:45.200
The carriage is on the right.

68
00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:50.400
Thread up the carriage
and carefully knit across.

69
00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.280
This will join the placket edges

70
00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:09.400
and continue knitting
the other side of the sweater.

71
00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:12.480
But first, we need to create the facing

72
00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:20.560
for the second side. The carriage is
on the left. E-wrap over 6 more needles.

73
00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:25.280
Again, these will be the facing for the
second side of the placket. As before.

74
00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:31.320
I recommend using a Cast-on rag
and ravel cord to make things easier.

75
00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.440
Knit 30 rows to complete the second side.

76
00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:01.960
And when the knitting comes off,
the machine is going to look like this.

77
00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:12.640
On the public side,
the knitting appears to be continuous.

78
00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:20.640
And when you peek inside,
you can see the ewrap edge.

79
00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:24.080
Neatly secure the facings by hand.

80
00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:30.760
And finish the neck edge as
desired, with the facings in place,

81
00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:34.120
if you're going to be adding this

82
00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:38.920
to a sweater, you'll need to make a plan.
Sketch out the depth of your placket

83
00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:45.160
opening, determine how wide
you want your  placket facings.

84
00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:49.760
Position it in relation
to your armhole shaping.

85
00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:52.960
In the sample, we just knit 30 rows.

86
00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:56.640
But for a sweater,
you'll need to knit to the neck,

87
00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:01.720
opening instructions in your pattern,
keeping in mind that you'll have those

88
00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:06.200
extra placket stitches to work
in as you finish your neckline.

